WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.600
What should a country do if another country&nbsp;
seizes one of its citizens in what looks like

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:16.000
hostage diplomacy? Sadly, this is not a hypothetical&nbsp;
question. Iran holds several western citizens on

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.840
flimsy charges. Muammar Gaddafi seized two Swiss&nbsp;
businessmen after Swiss authorities arrested

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.800
his son for battery. And just recently, China&nbsp;
effectively admitted to engaging in similar

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:32.480
hostage diplomacy, which is a form of gray-zone&nbsp;
warfare. One strategy is to do like Switzerland

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.680
did and suspend of all the visas issued to the&nbsp;
offending country. A more effective alternative

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:42.000
would be for the targeted country to suspend&nbsp;
the visas of selected members of the perpetrator

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:49.040
country's elite, including their families. Remember,&nbsp;
the children of the elite often live in the West.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:54.720
Another strategy would be a luxury goods embargo.&nbsp;
Suspend exports of designer brand clothes, shoes,

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.320
bags, and jewelry. It, too, would primarily hit the&nbsp;elite.
Yes, designer clothes have nothing to do with

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.756
hostage diplomacy, but in the gray-zone,&nbsp;
the key is to respond with surprising moves.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:11.280
Some of the ideas presented in this video&nbsp;
are from my new book, The Defender's Dilemma.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.720
To learn more, check the links provided. Also, let&nbsp;
